[THOUGHT] The diagnosis of malignant melanoma is directly confirmed by histopathology: “atypical melanocytes with concomitant epidermal and dermal infiltration — diagnostic of invasive malignant melanoma.” This is a definitive, gold-standard histologic criterion for invasive melanoma. All other differential diagnoses are robustly excluded by the same histologic description:  
- Metastatic carcinoma is ruled out because the atypical cells are melanocytic (not epithelial or lymphoid);  
- Spitzoid tumors are excluded by the pathologist’s explicit diagnostic statement (“diagnostic of invasive malignant melanoma”) and absence of spitzoid features in the summary;  
- pcALCL is excluded by lineage (melanocytic vs. lymphoid) and immunophenotypic expectations;  
- Blue nevus variants are excluded by the presence of *epidermal* atypical melanocytes (blue nevi are strictly dermal).  
Dermoscopy being “normal” is not contradictory—it aligns with amelanotic or early melanomas. The lesion’s asymptomatic, progressive growth is classic for melanoma. While Breslow thickness, ulceration, mitotic rate, and staging workup are pending, these are *prognostic and management parameters*, not diagnostic prerequisites—the diagnosis itself is already histologically confirmed. No red-flag data gaps impair diagnostic validity (e.g., no missing biopsy confirmation, no conflicting histology). Turn limit is 5/10—well within allowance—but decision does not hinge on turns here, as sufficiency and consistency are fully met.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

